WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.860
The last medical emergency we'll talk about is fainting.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:08.060
Fainting happens when blood flow to the brain suddenly drops, often triggered by things

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:12.100
like standing too long, dehydration, or even just getting overheated.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:17.180
If you see someone faint, your first step is to make sure the area is safe and help them lie

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:20.660
down flat on their back so you can check their breathing and responsiveness.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:24.040
If they seem too hot, take a moment to help cool them down.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.459
It is important to remember that you should avoid moving the person,

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:32.500
especially if you suspect a head or neck injury, unless the scene itself becomes dangerous

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.419
or you have to move them to perform life-saving CPR.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:39.419
You'll want to call 911 if they stay unresponsive for more than a minute

00:00:39.419 --> 00:00:42.580
or don't improve quickly, or if they were injured when they fell.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:47.400
Always call for professional help if an infant, young child, or pregnant person faints.

00:00:47.779 --> 00:00:52.480
To prevent a faint before it happens, if a person tells you they feel dizzy or lightheaded,

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.840
have them sit or lie down immediately.

00:00:55.279 --> 00:00:57.459
Crossing their legs or leaning over with their head

00:00:57.459 --> 00:01:00.700
between their knees can often help that dizzy-feeling pass.

